WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I can't speak for
every state in the union

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.505
about exactly what their
voting rights and laws are.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.774 align:start size:97% position:3%
But there are things in
that bill that are mandated

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.009
that Florida already does.

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.477 align:start size:81% position:19%
We already have
early voting here.

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:13.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have a 14-day period or
13-day period of early voting.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:16.516
And then you've got, anybody
can vote by mail in Florida.

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:17.717
You don't have to prove

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:18.985 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you're gonna be
out of town or whatever.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:20.620
And then you've
got election day,

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:21.554
where you can go
to your precinct

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:22.822
and from 7:00 am to 7:00 pm,

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.457
as long as you're in
line before 7:00 pm,

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:25.592
you can vote that way.

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:26.993
So it's never been easier.

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:30.030
But when I first ran
for office in 2010,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.433
there was still, you know,
early voting was at one point

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.734
in the state limited,

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.636
it didn't even exist
20 years ago, right.

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:39.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then when I first voted
in my first election 1989,

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.840
there was no early voting.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.308
If you wanted to vote absentee,

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.511 align:start position:0%
you had to write a letter
saying I'm gonna be out of town

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
or having surgery or
whatever it is on that day.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.983
And so, I just think
it's ever been easier

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:53.586
to either register or
to vote in Florida.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:56.156
(upbeat music)

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:01.961
- I'm Dave Rubin.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:05.265 line:0%
This is the Rubin report
with my first interview,

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:07.400 line:0%
from the free state of Florida.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.135
And we got the right guy for it,

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.371
Florida Senator Marco Rubio.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.439
Senator, how are you?

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.107
- I'm good.

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.241
Glad to have you here.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
And just a disclaimer
at the outset.

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.478
This is like real
world right here.

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:20.013
I'm in my backyard.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.215
I got my dog is right here, so
if you hear a little barking,

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.450
that's why I'm using these,

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.052
but, we're not trying
to stage anything here.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.187
It's all real man.

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:28.455
- There is...

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:29.656
- So, I apologize
ahead if he barks.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:30.824 align:start size:81% position:19%
- You're going
full Florida, man.

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:32.025
So first off

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:32.826
(Marco chuckles)

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:34.360
as you know,

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.429
I just moved here from the
crazy state of California.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:37.664
I've been here for
a couple of weeks,

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:38.832
just moved into the house.

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.366
I'm in my makeshift studio.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:44.170
Now, you are nice enough to do
a little promo video for me,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.672
welcoming me here.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.707
So let's just start with
the state of Florida first.

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.276
I mean, what is it about Florida

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.679
that sorta like allowed Florida

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:54.848
to do it right over
these last two years?

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:57.083
Like, I don't think it's
just the political part,

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.351
although that's a part of it.

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:00.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
What is it about just
sort of the Florida man,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.988
the Florida woman

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:02.922
- Yeah.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:05.191
- That did something right here.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.659
- That's a great question.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.128 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
I do think it starts with
political leadership,

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.964 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
but maybe the truth is that
that the people produce

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.165 line:0%
that political leadership.

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.700
They reward a certain
kind of leadership

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.201
and don't reward others.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know, I think the
key thing about Florida

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.638
and understand is
that everyone's

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.406
from somewhere else
for the most part.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that means another
part of the country

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:23.143
like you are married.

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.978
Hundreds of thousands
of people in Florida

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:26.579
that have done what you've done.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that is you came
here because you
didn't like what life

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.519
was like under the leadership
of people in some other state.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.487
And so, when people come
to a place to get away

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.856 align:start size:97% position:3%
from that kind of stuff
there, they came for a reason.

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.158
So I think that's
a big part of it.

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.659
And it's not just
from other states.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.227
We also have people that
come from other countries.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.562
So here in south Florida,

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:44.097
as I'm sure you've
already experienced,

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:46.432 align:start size:97% position:3%
we have a lot of people that
obviously came from Cuba

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.169
or their parents did and then
Venezuela and then Nicaragua.

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:52.405
And so any time that there is,
or for that matter, you know,

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.841
any time there's any sort
of a left-wing disruption

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:56.409
to Latin America.

00:02:56.409 --> 00:03:00.180
So for example, Peru elected
a president who was far left

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.714
and even anti-American,

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:04.384 align:start size:88% position:13%
and suddenly you see the
real estate purchases

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:05.785
from Peruvian skyrocket.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:08.421
People come here to get
away from that stuff.

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:09.756
They come here from
other countries,

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:10.990
they come here from other states

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.592 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I think that's
a big, big part

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:15.228 align:start size:84% position:16%
of what makes
Florida what it is.

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:17.330
And it's something we really
need to protect, you know.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.866
And it's a dynamic
state in many ways,

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.402
it's sort of a 21st century
frontier state in that way.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:24.504
- Well listen man, I
am here to protect it.

00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:25.705
As I keep telling people,

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.940
(Marco chuckles)

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:27.907
I'm here to keep
Florida, Florida.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.275
I have noticed, you know,

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:30.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
you mentioned the
international part of this.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:31.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, one thing I've
definitely noticed

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.146
in a couple of weeks here,

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:34.614
especially in the
Miami area, I mean,

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:37.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
everyone brings their
own driving customs here.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.985
There is a certain set

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:39.986
(Marco chuckles)

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:42.088
of ways that you
drive in Florida

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:44.691
that I have not seen
in California before.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.126
- Yeah.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.394
I mean, it's always the
game about, you know,

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
can I beat the red light
or is it, you know,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.365 align:start position:0%
it's all like a ten second rule
on the red light, you know,

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:54.467
like the light is red, but
does it really mean red?

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:55.735
(Dave chuckles)

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:56.903
Or do I get, it's
something like,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.137 align:start size:84% position:16%
you can drop something
on the floor,

00:03:58.137 --> 00:03:59.105 align:start size:84% position:16%
you get a two second
rule, whatever.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.873
Same thing with that, you know,

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.108
everybody's trying
to figure out how

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:04.210
to use a Backstreet to
cut-through traffic.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.945
There's a little bit
of that, you know,

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:07.981
and it's part of the
flare of the place.

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:09.482
I'm not encouraging
people to do it,

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:11.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it's part of the, sort
of the character of it.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.286
I think that's true
would probably more

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:15.421
in south Florida than in
other parts of the state,

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.691 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it is, yeah I
know it's different.

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.360
- I've definitely become a bit

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.594
(Marco chuckles)

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:22.528
of a Venezuelan or
Cuban driver already

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:23.463
in a couple of weeks here.

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.864
But in these last two years,

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
that Florida
continued to flourish

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.801
while so many states collapsed.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.604
And as you said, there are
quite literally hundreds

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:35.241
of thousands of people moving
in this direction right now.

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:39.412 align:start size:97% position:3%
What do you do to stop
people who made the mistakes

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:43.249 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the blue states from
repeating those mistakes here?

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:44.884 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not going to
repeat those mistakes,

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:46.986 align:start size:88% position:13%
but, you know, obviously
some people will.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:49.756
- Yeah.

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:51.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think first of all, it's,
self-selected, I mean,

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:52.625
you know, I'm not saying
that's true for everybody,

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.394 align:start size:84% position:16%
but frankly, the
bottom line is this,

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:57.597
if you live in California and
you watch MSNBC habitually

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.532
and you spend all
day on Twitter,

00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:01.200 align:start size:84% position:16%
you think that in
Florida we have bodies

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.669
all over the streets
and we have, you know,

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:04.237 align:start size:84% position:16%
people are just
dying left and right.

00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:06.973
And Governor DeSantis is
the leader of a death cult.

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:08.608
So you're not kind
of moved to Florida,

00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:09.809
if that's what you think,

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
unless your job makes
you or something.

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.446
So by and large people
that are moving here

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:14.681
are people that are,

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:16.549
they don't believe that
stuff when they come.

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:18.051 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, doesn't mean that's
true for everybody.

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:21.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
And look, well this is a
state with diverse opinions,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.257
but this has always been a
state that's had liberals.

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:25.758
This has always been a
state that's had people

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:28.828
that are left of center
on their political views,

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.797
but I don't think that's
the dividing line anymore.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:32.999
I mean, I think you can be
a liberal and not be crazy.

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:34.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think what we're talking
about now is things

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.402
that go well beyond
just traditional,

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:38.571
I want higher taxes
and more government.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.274 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is the complete
total abandonment

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:45.111
of common sense, of
balance of the reason

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:49.916
and almost so hysteria and its
approach to different issues.

00:05:49.916 --> 00:05:53.519
And I do think we've avoided
a lot of that happening here.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:55.722
It doesn't mean it's
not present at all,

00:05:55.722 --> 00:05:57.890
but I don't see a
of that happening,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.125
it doesn't mean, you know,

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there aren't people
that hold those views

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:01.294
or they talk in those ways,

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.196
but I think what's
really important is

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:04.564
to show the results, right.

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:05.798
At the end of the day,

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:08.201
there's a reason
why Ocasio-Cortez

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.436
and, you know, all
these other members

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:12.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
of Congress come
down here to vacation

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:14.540
and party on New Year's Eve.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.008
And it's because, you know,

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.277 align:start position:0%
they may be in favor of the
rules or they at least publicly

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:19.846
in favor of the rules
and their home state.

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:22.181
But when it comes time
to actually enjoy life,

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:23.649
they're coming here,

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:24.884
they're not going to some
of these other places.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, I think
reminding people that,

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
and comparing us to
all these other states

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:33.092 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what they're
going through is one

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:35.027 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the most important things
we can continue to do to,

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:36.863
to convince people
and win this battle.

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:40.032 align:start position:0%
- Do you ever envy the fact
that the Democrats can get away

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.834
with virtually anything,

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:45.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
like AOC can be partying
down here at clubs, you know,

00:06:45.972 --> 00:06:47.373
maskless and the whole thing

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:49.108
while she's locking
down her own people

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:52.011
when clearly, if you
were to do anything

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:53.613
that the media could figure out

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:56.416
in some slight way
was hypocritical,

00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:58.985
or when say, Senator
Cruz went on a vacation

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:02.221 align:start position:0%
that was long planned for a
couple of days, they go bananas,

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:04.257 align:start size:88% position:13%
but Democrats can
pretty much do anything.

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:07.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
Do you ever get a
little jealous of that?

00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:08.461
- You know, that's
a great question.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:09.996
And let me tell
you what happens.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:11.731
And I can talk to you about
my evolution on all of this.

00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:14.066
I think when you first kind of
get into politics, you know,

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:15.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
I got to like the 10
years ago and I get there

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.670
and I'm new to the
place and all that.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.173
And so, whether it's
the people around you

00:07:20.173 --> 00:07:21.407
or just your own instincts,

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:23.109
you sorta think you all
right, you guys, yes,

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:24.577
they're left of center
and they're liberal,

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:26.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
but at some point there's
some fairness in them, right.

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.782
And so, I think you
waste a lot of time

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:34.353 align:start position:0%
thinking you're going to impress
people in the legacy media.

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:36.289 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think that's worse
than it's ever been

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:39.325
in terms of their
bias and so forth.

00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:41.794
And I think maybe
one of the things

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.062
that Donald Trump revealed

00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:44.697
is you're never gonna
impress these people.

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:46.866 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, they're never
gonna treat you the same

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:48.267
as they do the other side.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:50.069
I think once you abandon that,

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:52.104
and once you accept
that that's a fact.

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:54.307
And then the next step
is realizing by the way,

00:07:54.307 --> 00:07:55.541
a lot of people notice,

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:56.676
like there's a lot
of people out there

00:07:56.676 --> 00:07:58.845
that notice this
double standard.

00:07:58.845 --> 00:08:00.713 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I always chuckle
about things like,

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:03.115 align:start size:94% position:6%
for example, you know,
look, I ran for president,

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:05.117
I finished in third place
in the delegate count,

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.586
I made it all the
way to Florida,

00:08:06.586 --> 00:08:07.954 align:start size:84% position:16%
It didn't turn out the
way I wanted it to,

00:08:07.954 --> 00:08:09.055
but that was God's plan

00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:10.590
for our country and for my life.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.025
And I, you know, there are a
lot of things I've been able

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.093
to do over the last four years.

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:15.595
I wouldn't have been able to do

00:08:15.595 --> 00:08:17.830
if I had won that race or
never been the nominee.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.833
And, you know, they call
me up for a long time,

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:24.170
They wrote about Marco Rubio,
failed presidential candidate.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.304
You know, Kamala
Harris didn't even

00:08:25.304 --> 00:08:26.572
make it out of California.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:27.773
(Dave chuckles)

00:08:27.773 --> 00:08:29.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
She didn't even
make it to December

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:30.543
- Polled it zero,
polled it zero.

00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:32.512
- Yeah, Cory Booker didn't
even make it to December.

00:08:32.512 --> 00:08:34.013
He didn't even make
it to the first stage

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:35.648
and they're called former
presidential candidates.

00:08:35.648 --> 00:08:37.250
So I'm a failed one,
they're a former one.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:38.885
I don't care, but
it's an example

00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:41.721
of that sort of double
standard and it exists.

00:08:41.721 --> 00:08:43.189
And I think there comes a point

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:44.657
where you kind of
realize, who cares,

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:46.158
like it doesn't really matter.

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:49.228
People see it.

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:50.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
And frankly, people
don't believe it anymore.

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:52.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're just not
living in an era now.

00:08:52.098 --> 00:08:54.500
There're more people that
will watch this podcast

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:55.968
then we'll read, you know,

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:58.938 align:start position:0%
what the local newspaper writes
about me on any given day.

00:08:58.938 --> 00:09:01.240
And once you sort
of realize that,

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.409
it makes life a lot
easier, you know.

00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:06.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, you know, so with
some of the COVID stuff here,

00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:09.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
because to your point,
if you watch MSNBC,

00:09:09.148 --> 00:09:10.616
they may, or read
the New York times,

00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:12.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
they make it sound like
everyone's dying in Florida.

00:09:12.852 --> 00:09:14.320
I'll tell you a
very straight up,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.456
I got COVID right when I got
here, my whole staff got COVID.

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:19.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was able to get
monoclonal treatment.

00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:20.760
It took literally about an hour.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.728
I was able to get ivermectin.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.298
I was sick for a couple of
days, and then I was better.

00:09:25.298 --> 00:09:26.766
It was just fine.

00:09:26.766 --> 00:09:27.967
That's not to say that
COVID doesn't exist here,

00:09:27.967 --> 00:09:29.468
of course it does.

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:32.305
But that's in stark contrast
to a place like California

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:35.608
or New York where you can't
really get those treatments.

00:09:35.608 --> 00:09:38.177
I mean, first off, they want
you to have a positive test

00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:39.445
to get the treatment,

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:41.147
but then you can't
even get the test.

00:09:41.147 --> 00:09:43.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
So when you guys were
putting together the policy

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:45.851
on all of this, and
when you were talking

00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:48.421
to the governor and the
other health officials,

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:50.823 align:start size:84% position:16%
how much pressure
did you feel to like,

00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:52.058
we don't want to
be the one state

00:09:52.058 --> 00:09:53.659 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is a little
more open, but then,

00:09:53.659 --> 00:09:56.095
we end up being the one
that really screws it up?

00:09:57.797 --> 00:09:59.298
- Well look, I mean,
there's a lot of credit

00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:00.533
to be deserved by the governor.

00:10:00.533 --> 00:10:02.802
I mean, he took
some chances here

00:10:02.802 --> 00:10:04.337
in terms of what was being told

00:10:04.337 --> 00:10:06.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
to him from, you know,
the national media

00:10:06.472 --> 00:10:08.174
versus what he decided to do

00:10:08.174 --> 00:10:11.210 align:start position:0%
on the basis of the information
he had and that move

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:16.215 align:start size:97% position:3%
to make monoclonal antibodies
available to people,

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:18.484
not just that they got sick,

00:10:18.484 --> 00:10:20.019
but even if they've
been exposed to it,

00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:21.554
I think saved a lot of lives

00:10:21.554 --> 00:10:23.522
and kept a lot of people out
of hospitals, it really did.

00:10:23.522 --> 00:10:26.892
And I know that firsthand from
people that went through it.

00:10:26.892 --> 00:10:30.096
I think that was a major
move, and an important one.

00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:32.398 line:0%
And why other states
didn't follow suit?

00:10:32.398 --> 00:10:33.933 line:0%
You know, I don't know.

00:10:33.933 --> 00:10:36.035 line:0%
I can tell you that why we
got into kind of involved

00:10:36.035 --> 00:10:37.536 line:0%
in it at that point is which,

00:10:37.536 --> 00:10:39.472 align:start position:0% line:0%
when the federal government
under Joe Biden realized Florida

00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:40.973
is making us look bad,

00:10:40.973 --> 00:10:42.808 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so, they decided we're
going to start rationing this.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.076
We're gonna start making sure

00:10:44.076 --> 00:10:46.012 align:start size:84% position:16%
that each state gets
a certain amount,

00:10:46.012 --> 00:10:47.546
but they don't, you know,

00:10:47.546 --> 00:10:49.982
even if they're not using it
a lot for them in essence,

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:52.585 align:start size:88% position:13%
a way to restrict
Florida's access to it.

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:54.387 align:start size:84% position:16%
And at least for
some of those drugs,

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:56.689
the state could directly
negotiate with a company.

00:10:56.689 --> 00:10:57.923
But there are some other,

00:10:57.923 --> 00:10:58.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
especially the newer
ones where you got to go

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:00.393
through the federal government.

00:11:00.393 --> 00:11:02.461 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's at the point
where we really got concerned

00:11:02.461 --> 00:11:03.829
because, you know,

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:05.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
when the federal
government starts saying,

00:11:05.064 --> 00:11:06.599
we're gonna start
rationing this,

00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:08.801
what you realize is they're
doing it to punish Florida,

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:10.236
they're doing it to make sure

00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:11.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Florida doesn't
continue to embarrass them

00:11:11.937 --> 00:11:14.540 align:start size:97% position:3%
by making this an effective
way to pursue this.

00:11:16.242 --> 00:11:17.977 align:start size:84% position:16%
But, yeah, you know,
look, ultimately,

00:11:17.977 --> 00:11:20.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's not just about
comparing us to other states.

00:11:20.179 --> 00:11:22.882 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, you don't want
anybody to die or get sick,

00:11:22.882 --> 00:11:25.551
but life is about the
management of risks.

00:11:25.551 --> 00:11:27.119
There's benefits,
there's rewards,

00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:28.320
and there's risks associated

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
to virtually
everything we'll do.

00:11:29.422 --> 00:11:31.057
At some point today, you and I

00:11:31.057 --> 00:11:33.259
and others will get in the
car and drive somewhere,

00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:35.428 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there's real risk
involved in that.

00:11:35.428 --> 00:11:37.463 align:start size:88% position:13%
And maybe more here than
other places, right.

00:11:37.463 --> 00:11:39.398
But, there's risk
involved in it.

00:11:39.398 --> 00:11:41.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
But, there are
things you just have

00:11:41.967 --> 00:11:43.135
to be able to do in life.

00:11:43.135 --> 00:11:45.805
And, in order to live.

00:11:45.805 --> 00:11:48.140
And I think once you
make public policy

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:49.408
from that perspective,

00:11:49.408 --> 00:11:52.078
it allows you to make
decisions like that.

00:11:52.078 --> 00:11:54.647
- Where are you at with
using the levers of power

00:11:54.647 --> 00:11:57.183 align:start size:84% position:16%
at this point, when
it comes to, you know,

00:11:57.183 --> 00:11:59.785
there's still a lot of kids
in masks in schools here,

00:11:59.785 --> 00:12:01.020
not all the schools,

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.655
it's a little unclear
to me if that's left

00:12:02.655 --> 00:12:04.990
to the counties at this
point, or the mayors,

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.059
or there's obviously some
level of school choice

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:09.328
and private, public
school, all of that,

00:12:09.328 --> 00:12:11.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
what do you think
should be happening?

00:12:11.297 --> 00:12:13.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
Are you trying to push
one way or another

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:14.734
or where you at?

00:12:15.935 --> 00:12:17.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, you know,
ultimately in my view,

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:19.905
I think one of the things
that this pandemic revealed

00:12:19.905 --> 00:12:21.340
is the importance
of local government

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.275
and local control to
make those decisions.

00:12:23.275 --> 00:12:24.810
And so, right now
at the state level,

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.912
schools are being told you
can't have a mask mandate.

00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:28.380
So as an example,

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.549 align:start size:97% position:3%
the public schools here at
Miami-Dade have a mask mandate

00:12:30.549 --> 00:12:32.651
for employees, but
not for the kids.

00:12:32.651 --> 00:12:34.153
And there's a lot of reasons

00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:35.721
that private schools can make
their own decisions, right.

00:12:35.721 --> 00:12:37.823
So I have one kid who was
in a public charter school

00:12:37.823 --> 00:12:39.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they don't have
a mask mandate,

00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:40.559
I have my oldest son

00:12:40.559 --> 00:12:42.895 align:start position:0%
is in a public private Catholic
high school, and they do.

00:12:42.895 --> 00:12:44.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I can tell you a
couple of things about it.

00:12:44.563 --> 00:12:46.232
The first is that, again,

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:48.701 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's a risk and reward
balancing prospect,

00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:50.002
let me be frank, right.

00:12:50.002 --> 00:12:52.505
The way these kids wear
the masks on, you know,

00:12:52.505 --> 00:12:55.608 align:start size:97% position:3%
12 to 20 hour basis whenever
we're around the school,

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:57.109
isn't exactly, you know,

00:12:57.109 --> 00:12:59.211 align:start size:84% position:16%
the way they're
supposed to wear them,

00:12:59.211 --> 00:13:01.380
if they're even effective
at all, in that sense.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:02.515
So that's the reality.

00:13:02.515 --> 00:13:03.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you start thinking
there's okay,

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:05.351
they're not gonna wear the mask

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:07.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
the way they're
supposed to anyway,

00:13:07.319 --> 00:13:09.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
all we're doing is creating
an additional irritant

00:13:09.622 --> 00:13:11.090
in their educational setting.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:13.125 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, you hope there's
some common sense

00:13:13.125 --> 00:13:14.794
into how that's enforced.

00:13:14.794 --> 00:13:17.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I think when you talk
about this Omicron variant,

00:13:17.196 --> 00:13:18.531
it ultimately isn't, I mean,

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:20.099 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you talk to
people who will speak

00:13:20.099 --> 00:13:21.400
to you honestly about it,

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:22.635
the banks aren't
gonna stop this,

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:24.403
this is a highly
infectious variant.

00:13:24.403 --> 00:13:27.673
Luckily it's not as severe,
but it is highly infectious.

00:13:27.673 --> 00:13:28.908
And there are just things

00:13:28.908 --> 00:13:30.409
that aren't gonna
work against it.

00:13:30.409 --> 00:13:34.113
And so, my view of it is,
I leave it to the states

00:13:34.113 --> 00:13:36.448 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the school boards
to make those decisions,

00:13:36.448 --> 00:13:38.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I would hope they
would make those decisions

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.286
with the best interests
of the kids in mind.

00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:42.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm much more worried
about them not learning how

00:13:42.621 --> 00:13:44.290
to read and write than I am

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.359
about them not wearing the
mask exactly right, you know,

00:13:47.359 --> 00:13:49.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the thing notched
over their nose.

00:13:49.628 --> 00:13:51.997
And, especially since
they're not gonna wear

00:13:51.997 --> 00:13:53.199
it right anyway,

00:13:53.199 --> 00:13:54.466
and the masks most
of the kids are using

00:13:54.466 --> 00:13:55.835 align:start size:88% position:13%
aren't even effective
against the old barrier,

00:13:55.835 --> 00:13:57.236
not to mention Omicron.

00:13:57.236 --> 00:13:58.204
- Yeah.

00:13:58.204 --> 00:13:59.438
Well, that's a perfect way

00:13:59.438 --> 00:14:00.906
to tie up the whole
Florida portion.

00:14:00.906 --> 00:14:03.275
So I want to move a little
bit towards national politics

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:06.445 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what's going on
right now at the moment.

00:14:06.445 --> 00:14:07.646
So in these last couple of days,

00:14:07.646 --> 00:14:10.382
obviously we saw the
stalemate in the Senate

00:14:10.382 --> 00:14:11.750
about the voting rights act.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.018
You're gonna call me crazy,

00:14:13.018 --> 00:14:14.653
I believe you have to have an ID

00:14:14.653 --> 00:14:16.255 align:start size:81% position:19%
or you should have
an ID to vote.

00:14:16.255 --> 00:14:18.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Democrats apparently,
don't believe that,

00:14:18.157 --> 00:14:20.292
and if you believe
it, you're racist.

00:14:20.292 --> 00:14:22.828
Now there's this fight
over the filibuster.

00:14:22.828 --> 00:14:24.096
You mentioned a few minutes ago

00:14:24.096 --> 00:14:26.932
that there used to sort
of be the same liberals

00:14:26.932 --> 00:14:29.168 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you could debate
these ideas with,

00:14:29.168 --> 00:14:31.070
is there anyone left over there?

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:32.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I suspect
you'd probably think

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:34.673
Joe Manchin's pretty
decent at the moment,

00:14:34.673 --> 00:14:37.543 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I mean, when you hear
them with these crazy policies

00:14:37.543 --> 00:14:38.978
where we're gonna
have illegal voting

00:14:38.978 --> 00:14:40.279
and you don't have to
have an ID to vote,

00:14:40.279 --> 00:14:42.314
but you also have to
have a vaccine card

00:14:42.314 --> 00:14:43.816
to walk into McDonald's.

00:14:43.816 --> 00:14:45.985 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, do you get behind
closed doors with these guys

00:14:45.985 --> 00:14:47.519
and go, what are you doing?

00:14:49.021 --> 00:14:51.724
- Yeah, look, I think there's
more than just Joe Manchin

00:14:51.724 --> 00:14:53.425
and Kyrsten Sinema
that they don't want

00:14:53.425 --> 00:14:54.960
to get rid of the filibuster

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.963
and think some of these ideas
go beyond their comfort level.

00:14:57.963 --> 00:14:59.431
But if you watch what's happened

00:14:59.431 --> 00:15:02.067
to Joe Manchin and Kyrsten
Sinema when they stepped out,

00:15:02.067 --> 00:15:04.403
there's a lot of people that
are saying, you know what,

00:15:04.403 --> 00:15:05.671
I'm just gonna vote with a pack

00:15:05.671 --> 00:15:06.906
and sort of keep my head down

00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:08.807
and it's not gonna pass anyway,

00:15:08.807 --> 00:15:10.776
thank God for Kyrsten
Sinema and Joe Manchin.

00:15:10.776 --> 00:15:12.711 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, these are people
that are voting yes,

00:15:12.711 --> 00:15:14.179
but hoping for no.

00:15:14.179 --> 00:15:16.715 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the fact that two people
are willing to step out

00:15:16.715 --> 00:15:18.384 align:start size:97% position:3%
and do it kind of gave them
the result they wanted,

00:15:18.384 --> 00:15:20.586
but these guys don't, they
don't want to be singled out.

00:15:20.586 --> 00:15:23.355 align:start position:0%
They don't want donors
threatening them with primaries,

00:15:23.355 --> 00:15:24.757
they're, scared.

00:15:24.757 --> 00:15:26.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, not saying
that's true of everybody,

00:15:26.892 --> 00:15:28.060
but yeah, there are Democrats

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.295
that view this as kind of crazy,

00:15:29.295 --> 00:15:30.529
but they got to say it

00:15:30.529 --> 00:15:32.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
because their base has
become radicalized.

00:15:32.131 --> 00:15:34.900
The people that knock on
doors and make phone calls

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:37.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that they need to
mobilize for election day

00:15:37.703 --> 00:15:40.839
are demanding whatever the
pet issue of the moment is.

00:15:40.839 --> 00:15:43.008 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there are far
outside the mainstream,

00:15:43.008 --> 00:15:45.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
but if they take those people
on, they may not be back

00:15:45.644 --> 00:15:46.578
and they certainly don't want

00:15:46.578 --> 00:15:48.147
the headache associated with it.

00:15:48.147 --> 00:15:49.581 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, I think as
much as anything else,

00:15:49.581 --> 00:15:51.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
the threats against Kyrsten
Sinema and Joe Manchin

00:15:51.817 --> 00:15:53.719
that you've seen, is not so much

00:15:53.719 --> 00:15:55.154 align:start size:84% position:16%
about Kyrsten Sinema
and Joe Manchin,

00:15:55.154 --> 00:15:57.022
it's a message to
the other senators

00:15:57.022 --> 00:15:59.591 align:start size:97% position:3%
and other members of
Congress who may be thinking

00:15:59.591 --> 00:16:00.659
about stepping out of line,

00:16:00.659 --> 00:16:02.161
that this is what awaits you,

00:16:02.161 --> 00:16:05.597
this is what lies ahead for
you, if you step out of line.

00:16:05.597 --> 00:16:08.500
So just be quiet and do
what we tell you to do.

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:10.636
And that's what I'd describe it.

00:16:10.636 --> 00:16:13.672 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Are you surprised that the
leadership folded kind of

00:16:13.672 --> 00:16:14.840
as quickly as it did?

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:16.208
Because I'm sure you saw it,

00:16:16.208 --> 00:16:17.543 align:start size:88% position:13%
you may have been in
the chamber at the time.

00:16:17.543 --> 00:16:19.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
Tom Cotton did a
really spectacular job

00:16:19.979 --> 00:16:22.414
of repeating a speech
that Chuck Schumer gave

00:16:22.414 --> 00:16:24.283
about the filibuster.

00:16:24.283 --> 00:16:26.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think it was from, you
know, five or eight years ago,

00:16:26.218 --> 00:16:27.453
something like that.

00:16:27.453 --> 00:16:29.121
And then he announced
at the end, by the way,

00:16:29.121 --> 00:16:31.357
that was Chuck Schumer,
that wasn't my speech.

00:16:31.357 --> 00:16:32.858
And it's like,

00:16:32.858 --> 00:16:34.693
I don't think Chuck was
truly this radically left

00:16:34.693 --> 00:16:37.062 align:start size:84% position:16%
and even Nancy Pelosi
in San Francisco,

00:16:37.062 --> 00:16:39.298
like obviously she
had some of that,

00:16:39.298 --> 00:16:41.066
but I don't think she
was totally like this.

00:16:41.066 --> 00:16:42.835
Are you surprised
that they folded,

00:16:42.835 --> 00:16:44.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
not just the guys
that are watching out

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:45.904
for their back and just
want to get reelected,

00:16:45.904 --> 00:16:48.774 align:start size:88% position:13%
but that the leadership
just all went in.

00:16:48.774 --> 00:16:49.942
- On the filibuster?

00:16:49.942 --> 00:16:50.876
- Well, on the filibuster

00:16:50.876 --> 00:16:52.711
and that they're following just,

00:16:52.711 --> 00:16:55.614 align:start size:88% position:13%
they're sort of far left
on all of this stuff.

00:16:55.614 --> 00:16:57.249
- I'm not actually surprised.

00:16:57.249 --> 00:16:59.084
And I'll tell you why.

00:16:59.084 --> 00:17:02.021 align:start position:0%
So, they have all, here's what,
they've got a 50/50 Senate,

00:17:02.021 --> 00:17:04.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
a very narrow
majority in the house,

00:17:04.623 --> 00:17:06.959
but their base thinks
they have a mandate

00:17:06.959 --> 00:17:09.194 align:start size:97% position:3%
to like transform radically
transform America.

00:17:09.194 --> 00:17:11.230
And so they're getting
all this pressure about,

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:13.332
get this stuff done and
you go back then and say,

00:17:13.332 --> 00:17:15.434
well, we can't, we
don't have 60 votes.

00:17:15.434 --> 00:17:16.668
And then the base says to them,

00:17:16.668 --> 00:17:17.936 align:start size:88% position:13%
well then, get rid of
the 60 vote thing, okay.

00:17:17.936 --> 00:17:19.371
'Cause it's standing in the way

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:20.672
of our agenda and get it done.

00:17:20.672 --> 00:17:21.840
And if you don't get it done

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:23.142 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that means
you're not with us.

00:17:23.142 --> 00:17:25.177
And so, after a while
they start beating on,

00:17:25.177 --> 00:17:28.013
you still look at this
fake voting rights thing.

00:17:28.013 --> 00:17:29.715
It's just the issue they picked

00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:31.583
to have a filibuster fight over,

00:17:31.583 --> 00:17:33.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they were prepared to
do it over the debt ceiling.

00:17:33.619 --> 00:17:35.187
And they were at
some point talking

00:17:35.187 --> 00:17:37.890 align:start size:97% position:3%
about doing it for immigration
reform, you name it,

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:39.324
and they would find the issue.

00:17:39.324 --> 00:17:41.527 align:start position:0%
This is just the one they
figured we have the best chance

00:17:41.527 --> 00:17:44.296
to make a straight
faced argument on,

00:17:44.296 --> 00:17:45.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
even though it wasn't
a very good one.

00:17:45.931 --> 00:17:47.433
And as holding on it,

00:17:47.433 --> 00:17:49.535
I don't think Chuck Schumer
had a choice, I really don't.

00:17:49.535 --> 00:17:51.503 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think Chuck Schumer
hadn't done this,

00:17:51.503 --> 00:17:53.338 align:start size:88% position:13%
he was almost certainly
gonna be primary

00:17:53.338 --> 00:17:55.207
in New York in his Senate race.

00:17:55.207 --> 00:17:56.508 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I think he
thought about that

00:17:56.508 --> 00:17:58.610
as he went through this cycle.

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:00.479
And I think that's true for
a lot of their leadership,

00:18:00.479 --> 00:18:02.815
and a lot of the people that
changed their position on it.

00:18:02.815 --> 00:18:04.550
At the end of the day, yes,
they're in the leadership,

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:07.519
but they need to win elections,
and stay in that leadership.

00:18:07.519 --> 00:18:09.321 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in that case,
the case in New York,

00:18:09.321 --> 00:18:11.023
it means the Democratic primary

00:18:11.023 --> 00:18:12.591 align:start size:88% position:13%
where he most
definitely does not want

00:18:12.591 --> 00:18:15.160
to take on a far left
primary challenger

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:16.595
that could beat him,
that could beat him.

00:18:16.595 --> 00:18:18.130 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, he's trying
to avoid that.

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:20.399
And I think it was
pretty clear to him

00:18:20.399 --> 00:18:22.034
that he didn't do this,

00:18:22.034 --> 00:18:22.901 align:start size:84% position:16%
that was what's gonna
to happen to him.

00:18:22.901 --> 00:18:24.369
- Yeah.

00:18:24.369 --> 00:18:26.271 align:start size:97% position:3%
And there were even rumors
that AOC might be that person.

00:18:26.271 --> 00:18:27.773
Just to give the devil his due.

00:18:27.773 --> 00:18:30.309
I mean, was there anything
in the voting rights act

00:18:30.309 --> 00:18:32.311
that you thought was legit,

00:18:32.311 --> 00:18:34.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
or do you think the
entire thing was designed

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
for all the mail in ballots,
all the no ID stuff,

00:18:37.416 --> 00:18:39.585
all the illegals, like
was it all designed just

00:18:39.585 --> 00:18:40.619
to keep them in power forever

00:18:40.619 --> 00:18:42.054
or was there anything in there?

00:18:42.054 --> 00:18:44.223 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, is there
anyone in America today

00:18:44.223 --> 00:18:46.091 align:start size:81% position:19%
that wants to vote
that can't vote

00:18:46.091 --> 00:18:47.493
for a legitimate reason

00:18:47.493 --> 00:18:48.794 align:start size:88% position:13%
or for any illegitimate
reason, as you say?

00:18:48.794 --> 00:18:50.062
- I don't think it's ever,

00:18:50.062 --> 00:18:52.965
look, I can't speak for
every state in the union

00:18:52.965 --> 00:18:55.534
about exactly what their
voting rights and laws are,

00:18:55.534 --> 00:18:57.069 align:start size:84% position:16%
but there are
things in that bill

00:18:57.069 --> 00:18:59.004
that are mandated that
Florida already does.

00:18:59.004 --> 00:19:01.373
We already have early
voting here, okay.

00:19:01.373 --> 00:19:03.642 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have a 14-day period of
13-day period of early voting.

00:19:03.642 --> 00:19:06.512
And then you've got, anybody
can vote by mail in Florida.

00:19:06.512 --> 00:19:08.013
You don't have to prove
that you're gonna be out

00:19:08.013 --> 00:19:08.981
of town or whatever.

00:19:08.981 --> 00:19:10.282
And then you got election day

00:19:10.282 --> 00:19:11.216
where you can go
to your precinct

00:19:11.216 --> 00:19:12.684
and from 7:00 am to 7:00 pm

00:19:12.684 --> 00:19:14.453
as long as you're in
line before 7:00 pm,

00:19:14.453 --> 00:19:15.587
you can vote that way.

00:19:15.587 --> 00:19:16.989
So it's never been easier.

00:19:16.989 --> 00:19:20.325
But, when I first ran
for office in 2010,

00:19:20.325 --> 00:19:21.793
there was still, you know,

00:19:21.793 --> 00:19:24.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
early voting was at one
point in this state limited.

00:19:24.229 --> 00:19:26.598
They didn't even exist
20 years ago, right.

00:19:26.598 --> 00:19:29.468
It didn't, when I first voted
in my first election, 1989,

00:19:29.468 --> 00:19:30.903
there was no early voting.

00:19:30.903 --> 00:19:32.337
If you wanted to vote absentee,

00:19:32.337 --> 00:19:34.473 align:start position:0%
you had to write a letter
saying I'm gonna be out of town

00:19:34.473 --> 00:19:36.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
or having surgery or
whatever it is on that day.

00:19:36.975 --> 00:19:39.945
And so, I don't think
it's ever been easier

00:19:39.945 --> 00:19:43.448
to either register or
to vote in Florida.

00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:46.518 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, it's not, for me, the
big problem is first of all,

00:19:46.518 --> 00:19:50.689
there is no concerted effort
to deny access to the ballot,

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:51.990
to anyone in America.

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:54.526
Some of those ideas that
are talking about, okay,

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:56.261
you know, ballot harvesting,

00:19:56.261 --> 00:19:57.396
allowing people to go out

00:19:57.396 --> 00:19:59.031 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just grab
ballots and put them,

00:19:59.031 --> 00:20:02.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
we had a huge crisis about
that here in Florida.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:03.502
And by the way,

00:20:03.502 --> 00:20:05.170
it was in a lot of times in
Republican primary races,

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:06.505 align:start size:84% position:16%
where were these
people you would hire

00:20:06.505 --> 00:20:07.773
and they would go out

00:20:07.773 --> 00:20:09.875
and they would collect
the 150, 200 ballots.

00:20:09.875 --> 00:20:12.177
And, it just led to all
sorts of shenanigans.

00:20:12.177 --> 00:20:14.813
Look, we had a mayor's
race in Miami overturned

00:20:14.813 --> 00:20:17.349
by a judge a year
after the election,

00:20:17.349 --> 00:20:19.051
because there was so
much massive fraud

00:20:19.051 --> 00:20:20.252
on the absentee ballot front.

00:20:20.252 --> 00:20:23.255
A lot of it driven
by ballot harvesting.

00:20:23.255 --> 00:20:25.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, we banned those
practices here in Florida.

00:20:25.757 --> 00:20:27.759
And the biggest challenge,

00:20:27.759 --> 00:20:28.994
the biggest problem I had

00:20:28.994 --> 00:20:30.395
is this sort of the
federal government

00:20:30.395 --> 00:20:31.964
is going to take over elections,

00:20:31.964 --> 00:20:35.000
which is what this is,
is a huge problem for me

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.368
big, big problem.

00:20:36.368 --> 00:20:38.170 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because you've now
centralized election authority

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.039 align:start size:97% position:3%
in America and now it's at the
whim of whoever's in power.

00:20:42.007 --> 00:20:43.175
- Well, how worried are you that

00:20:43.175 --> 00:20:44.776
that is the future of America?

00:20:44.776 --> 00:20:47.312 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, my main concern
as a new Floridian

00:20:47.312 --> 00:20:50.115
is that we're gonna keep
doing things right here,

00:20:50.115 --> 00:20:51.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're gonna keep electing
the right officials,

00:20:51.717 --> 00:20:53.151
our businesses are
going to thrive,

00:20:53.151 --> 00:20:54.686
people are going to
be out and about,

00:20:54.686 --> 00:20:56.888
but the federal government
simply will not let us alone.

00:20:56.888 --> 00:20:58.457 align:start size:81% position:19%
It's sort of to
your point earlier

00:20:58.457 --> 00:21:00.559
about Florida did it right
with monoclonal treatment.

00:21:00.559 --> 00:21:02.427
So what was the federal
government response?

00:21:02.427 --> 00:21:04.229
Oh, we're gonna push tamper down

00:21:04.229 --> 00:21:06.331
on who can get it and
when they can get it.

00:21:06.331 --> 00:21:08.734 align:start size:84% position:16%
So what do we do here
to make sure that

00:21:08.734 --> 00:21:11.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
that sort of ever-growing
federal government

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:12.404
doesn't do what?

00:21:12.404 --> 00:21:13.805
I can't even imagine
what they could do,

00:21:13.805 --> 00:21:16.041
but I know there's
stuff they can do.

00:21:16.041 --> 00:21:17.276
- Yeah.

00:21:17.276 --> 00:21:18.744 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, that's a great
question to a Senator

00:21:18.744 --> 00:21:20.012
because if you look at
the design of the Senate,

00:21:20.012 --> 00:21:22.381
the Senate was designed
to represent the states,

00:21:22.381 --> 00:21:23.815
to be the voice of state.

00:21:23.815 --> 00:21:25.350 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's why every state
gets two of them.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:27.886
Small states get two of them,
big states to get two of them.

00:21:27.886 --> 00:21:30.489
And part of our job is to
keep that balance in place

00:21:30.489 --> 00:21:32.457
between the federal
and state government,

00:21:32.457 --> 00:21:34.593
between the different
levels of government.

00:21:34.593 --> 00:21:36.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, oftentimes
people think it's not,

00:21:36.862 --> 00:21:38.297
because I'm against

00:21:38.297 --> 00:21:39.865 align:start size:97% position:3%
the federal government
doing something on an issue,

00:21:39.865 --> 00:21:41.233 align:start size:84% position:16%
doesn't mean the issue
is not important,

00:21:41.233 --> 00:21:42.567
I just think the
local government

00:21:42.567 --> 00:21:44.269
or the state government
would do a better job

00:21:44.269 --> 00:21:46.305
of dealing with it than the
federal government would.

00:21:46.305 --> 00:21:47.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's actually a
fascinating question.

00:21:47.806 --> 00:21:50.275 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
If you go back to 1500,
from the year 1500 forward,

00:21:50.275 --> 00:21:52.944 line:0%
and you look at the rise
and fall of great powers,

00:21:52.944 --> 00:21:54.980 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
there are a lot of reasons
why great powers fall.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:56.281 line:0%
But one of the thing many

00:21:56.281 --> 00:21:57.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
of those great
powers had in common

00:21:57.783 --> 00:21:59.985
is they centralized
government control.

00:21:59.985 --> 00:22:03.355 align:start size:97% position:3%
And what that meant is that
entrepreneurial spirit,

00:22:03.355 --> 00:22:05.624
that competition to see
who had better ideas,

00:22:05.624 --> 00:22:08.593
that creativity and innovation
that you would see when power

00:22:08.593 --> 00:22:11.630
is decentralized, they
lost that, they lost it.

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:13.565
And the places that are a
little bit more flights,

00:22:13.565 --> 00:22:15.934
one of the key reasons
why Europe surpassed the,

00:22:15.934 --> 00:22:19.104
the Asia-Pacific region
in global leadership,

00:22:19.104 --> 00:22:21.740
because they didn't have a
strong central government

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:23.175
that sort of quashed that.

00:22:23.175 --> 00:22:24.710 align:start size:84% position:16%
So there's historical
reasons for it.

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:26.178
I think it's true with our,

00:22:26.178 --> 00:22:27.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's also aligned
with our constitution

00:22:27.679 --> 00:22:29.781
and look at every innovation

00:22:29.781 --> 00:22:32.117 align:start size:97% position:3%
in public education America
over the last 20 years,

00:22:32.117 --> 00:22:34.519
they've all been driven
by state initiatives

00:22:34.519 --> 00:22:36.988
like school choice and
things of that nature.

00:22:36.988 --> 00:22:38.223
They've all been driven

00:22:38.223 --> 00:22:40.359
by at the state level
because states want

00:22:40.359 --> 00:22:42.761
to have a better education
system than other states

00:22:42.761 --> 00:22:44.496
so that people will
move their businesses

00:22:44.496 --> 00:22:45.764
and their families there.

00:22:45.764 --> 00:22:48.300
And so, I'm gonna work very hard

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.168
to continue to make sure
that the Senate stands

00:22:50.168 --> 00:22:53.004
in the way of any effort
to have federal takeovers

00:22:53.004 --> 00:22:55.474
and big public policy
issues of that type.

00:22:55.474 --> 00:22:57.576
- So speaking of
federal takeovers,

00:22:57.576 --> 00:23:00.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
Biden obviously gave
this big press conference

00:23:00.045 --> 00:23:02.347
in the last couple of days.

00:23:02.347 --> 00:23:03.882
From where I'm sitting.

00:23:03.882 --> 00:23:07.285
I mean, the guy is not the
Joe Biden of 10 years ago.

00:23:07.285 --> 00:23:10.989 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that's a pretty deep
underestimation actually.

00:23:12.324 --> 00:23:14.860 align:start position:0%
Do you think he's really
running the show at this point?

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:16.294
I mean, he constantly,

00:23:16.294 --> 00:23:18.964 align:start position:0%
at the end of these press
conferences, he constantly says,

00:23:18.964 --> 00:23:20.499
oh, I'm not allowed to say that,

00:23:20.499 --> 00:23:21.900 align:start size:81% position:19%
or they don't want
me to say this,

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:25.303
or they brush him off or
he sits there awkwardly

00:23:25.303 --> 00:23:27.372
as people yell questions
and he doesn't answer.

00:23:27.372 --> 00:23:30.075 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, what do you
think is going on here?

00:23:30.075 --> 00:23:31.543
- Yeah.

00:23:31.543 --> 00:23:32.778
I mean, I don't know Joe
Biden very well personally.

00:23:32.778 --> 00:23:34.279
I never served with
him in the Senate.

00:23:34.279 --> 00:23:36.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
I did interact with him
when he was vice-president

00:23:36.615 --> 00:23:38.016
and obviously, you
know, the person

00:23:38.016 --> 00:23:40.619
that's there today is
not the same person.

00:23:40.619 --> 00:23:43.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I'm in no
position to speculate

00:23:43.021 --> 00:23:44.790 align:start size:88% position:13%
as to what has changed,
but it's just not.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:47.259
And that said, I mean,
he has a long history

00:23:47.259 --> 00:23:49.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
of sort of saying things
that are silly and gas-prone,

00:23:49.694 --> 00:23:52.097
we know that, I mean, that's
a well understood dynamic.

00:23:52.097 --> 00:23:53.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think the Obama
administration understood

00:23:53.698 --> 00:23:55.066
that about him as well,

00:23:55.066 --> 00:23:58.003 align:start size:84% position:16%
and tried to protect
him from himself

00:23:58.003 --> 00:24:00.505
and certainly administration
from some of those things.

00:24:00.505 --> 00:24:02.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well I think it really
becomes relevant,

00:24:02.774 --> 00:24:04.075
look, I'm not one of
these people that,

00:24:04.075 --> 00:24:05.544
because someone
misstates a word,

00:24:05.544 --> 00:24:07.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
it doesn't perfectly
phrase something,

00:24:07.078 --> 00:24:08.346
something goes crazy,

00:24:08.346 --> 00:24:09.281
I'll look at what they
said, and I'm gonna go

00:24:09.281 --> 00:24:10.749
that's silly sort of theatrics

00:24:10.749 --> 00:24:12.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I talk to the camera
for a living, I'm with ya,

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:12.984
I'm with ya.

00:24:12.984 --> 00:24:14.052
- Yeah.

00:24:14.052 --> 00:24:15.287
(Marco chuckles)

00:24:15.287 --> 00:24:16.488
So, I'm not big on
that kind of stuff,

00:24:16.488 --> 00:24:18.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I do think it's
relevant, okay.

00:24:18.356 --> 00:24:21.359
When you watch this and you
see a consistent pattern

00:24:21.359 --> 00:24:22.861
of saying things, you know,

00:24:22.861 --> 00:24:25.130
in the big speech he gave in
Georgia a couple of weeks ago,

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:26.398
he talked about when
he had been arrested,

00:24:26.398 --> 00:24:27.933
it's been well-documented,

00:24:27.933 --> 00:24:30.101
that he's never been arrested
and the civil rights protest.

00:24:30.101 --> 00:24:31.870
And I think if Vladimir Putin

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:34.339
or the Chinese leadership
of the communist party,

00:24:34.339 --> 00:24:35.774
you're watching that and saying,

00:24:35.774 --> 00:24:37.275 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's something
wrong with this guy.

00:24:37.275 --> 00:24:39.244
I mean, I think there are
things we can get away with

00:24:39.244 --> 00:24:40.679
as long as that guy is there.

00:24:40.679 --> 00:24:42.314 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the fact that they
would have the same question

00:24:42.314 --> 00:24:44.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you have, who's
actually running the place?

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:46.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
Who's actually
making the decisions?

00:24:46.785 --> 00:24:49.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is a dangerous thing
for the country.

00:24:49.254 --> 00:24:50.922
I mean, they had
to spend basically

00:24:50.922 --> 00:24:53.124 align:start size:88% position:13%
the last 24 hours
cleaning up what he said

00:24:53.124 --> 00:24:55.026
about Ukraine and, you know,

00:24:55.026 --> 00:24:57.028 align:start size:84% position:16%
a partial invasion
versus a full invasion

00:24:57.028 --> 00:25:00.031 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's just one element of
something he said on that day.

00:25:00.031 --> 00:25:02.801
The other is, he kinda
alluded, said, well,

00:25:02.801 --> 00:25:04.703
maybe a midterms aren't
gonna be legitimate,

00:25:04.703 --> 00:25:06.571
they had to clean
that up as well.

00:25:06.571 --> 00:25:10.141
So, you know, he's
not the same person,

00:25:10.141 --> 00:25:11.409
I don't know who's
running the show.

00:25:11.409 --> 00:25:12.844
I'm in no position to
speculate about that,

00:25:12.844 --> 00:25:13.778
but I can tell you,

00:25:13.778 --> 00:25:15.280
they spent a lot of time having

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:17.415
to sort of clean up
the things he says

00:25:17.415 --> 00:25:19.284
and our adversaries noticed it.

00:25:19.284 --> 00:25:20.452
- Yeah, for sure.

00:25:20.452 --> 00:25:21.620 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, look, we've
already talked

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:23.088 align:start size:88% position:13%
about the election
stuff so we can let part

00:25:23.088 --> 00:25:24.723
of what he said go
because it's sorta,

00:25:24.723 --> 00:25:25.957
(Dave chuckles)

00:25:25.957 --> 00:25:27.192
it's basically like
they spent four years

00:25:27.192 --> 00:25:28.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
under Trump
questioning elections.

00:25:28.693 --> 00:25:30.695 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now we can't, now
they're preparing us

00:25:30.695 --> 00:25:32.397 align:start size:88% position:13%
to question elections
when they get, you know,

00:25:32.397 --> 00:25:33.865
she'll act at the
end of this year.

00:25:33.865 --> 00:25:36.468
But on the Putin-Ukraine stuff,

00:25:36.468 --> 00:25:37.669
I thought that was one

00:25:37.669 --> 00:25:40.105 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the worst few minutes
a president has had

00:25:40.105 --> 00:25:41.206
at least in my lifetime.

00:25:41.206 --> 00:25:42.774
But basically just saying,

00:25:42.774 --> 00:25:44.142
you can kind of
do what you want.

00:25:44.142 --> 00:25:45.644
We don't really know.

00:25:45.644 --> 00:25:47.445 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, you pretty much know
we're not gonna do anything.

00:25:47.445 --> 00:25:49.648 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, what happened
to some level

00:25:49.648 --> 00:25:54.653 align:start position:0%
of deterrence or a red line
perhaps or something like that?

00:25:56.388 --> 00:25:57.622
- Yeah.

00:25:57.622 --> 00:25:59.090 align:start size:84% position:16%
Look, I mean, the
most important thing

00:25:59.090 --> 00:26:00.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
is you have to explain to
people why that even matters.

00:26:00.025 --> 00:26:01.393
That's a very legitimate point

00:26:01.393 --> 00:26:02.961 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause I think
Americans are very wary

00:26:02.961 --> 00:26:05.363
of sort of engaging us in
conflicts around the world.

00:26:05.363 --> 00:26:06.565
So let me be frank.

00:26:06.565 --> 00:26:07.799 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, there's not
gonna be American troops

00:26:07.799 --> 00:26:09.801
in Ukraine taking on
a Russian invasion

00:26:09.801 --> 00:26:11.903
because you can't really
have a conventional war

00:26:11.903 --> 00:26:13.238
between two nuclear powers.

00:26:13.238 --> 00:26:15.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's too risky,
it has the real danger

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:17.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
of escalating and the
battlefield into something,

00:26:17.709 --> 00:26:20.111 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's just not, that's not
what anybody's talking about.

00:26:20.111 --> 00:26:21.346
I think what is important

00:26:21.346 --> 00:26:22.614
is Vladimir Putin
has to assist there

00:26:22.614 --> 00:26:24.115
and we've got to do
everything possible

00:26:24.115 --> 00:26:26.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
so that he calculates that
the price of doing this

00:26:26.217 --> 00:26:28.486
is higher than the benefits
he's gonna gain from it.

00:26:28.486 --> 00:26:29.421
And that's not an
easy thing to do

00:26:29.421 --> 00:26:31.423
because the guy's convinced

00:26:31.423 --> 00:26:34.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we're gonna make
Ukraine a member of NATO,

00:26:34.359 --> 00:26:35.827
there are gonna be
American missile

00:26:35.827 --> 00:26:38.330
and troops there at some
point, right on his border.

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:40.065
And so, we've gotta change,

00:26:40.065 --> 00:26:42.200
that's how high the
risk is for him.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:44.135
We've gotta change the
reward pattern on it.

00:26:44.135 --> 00:26:45.804
I think part of
that is economic.

00:26:45.804 --> 00:26:47.072
I think part of it
is you're starting

00:26:47.072 --> 00:26:48.373
to hear people say
it as the reality,

00:26:48.373 --> 00:26:50.141
that Ukrainians aren't
gonna welcome these guys

00:26:50.141 --> 00:26:52.077
and say, oh thank you for
taking over our country.

00:26:52.077 --> 00:26:55.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, they're gonna face
a protracted insurgency

00:26:55.146 --> 00:26:57.816
of Ukrainian, non-Americans
of Ukrainians, you know,

00:26:57.816 --> 00:26:59.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know what
significant percentage

00:26:59.517 --> 00:27:02.887
of that population has a history

00:27:02.887 --> 00:27:05.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
or training or background
in military service.

00:27:05.724 --> 00:27:07.359
And you're gonna have, you know,

00:27:07.359 --> 00:27:09.828
years and years and years of
people taking shots at you.

00:27:09.828 --> 00:27:13.064
And I think this country
as much as any understands

00:27:13.064 --> 00:27:14.666
that when you invade a country,

00:27:14.666 --> 00:27:17.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
you now have to
govern and occupy it.

00:27:17.068 --> 00:27:18.903
And the people there
aren't that happy,

00:27:18.903 --> 00:27:20.505 align:start size:81% position:19%
they make life
miserable for you.

00:27:20.505 --> 00:27:21.873
It's really, it's gonna bleed

00:27:21.873 --> 00:27:24.075 align:start size:84% position:16%
and drain the
Russian government.

00:27:24.075 --> 00:27:25.777
And Vladimir Putin is
gonna have to explain

00:27:25.777 --> 00:27:28.513 align:start size:97% position:3%
to Russian moms why their
sons are coming home in boxes

00:27:29.914 --> 00:27:32.484
because they took over a
place that doesn't want em.

00:27:32.484 --> 00:27:34.686
And those risks have to
be made clear up front

00:27:34.686 --> 00:27:37.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
in addition to the economic
pain and suffering.

00:27:37.389 --> 00:27:41.326
I believe Vladimir Putin
is gonna do something.

00:27:41.326 --> 00:27:43.395
I think probably the
highest probability

00:27:43.395 --> 00:27:44.796
is he moves straight into KIA,

00:27:44.796 --> 00:27:47.098 align:start size:97% position:3%
goes right after KIA
decapitates the government,

00:27:47.098 --> 00:27:49.034
he's already setting
up the pretext for it.

00:27:49.034 --> 00:27:52.203
And we didn't need to make
sure that if he does it,

00:27:52.203 --> 00:27:54.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
he pays a tremendous
price 'cause it's
not just about Putin.

00:27:54.572 --> 00:27:57.175
This matters because if
he can get away with it,

00:27:57.175 --> 00:27:59.544 align:start size:88% position:13%
China says, well, that's
the model for Taiwan.

00:27:59.544 --> 00:28:01.279
And, maybe North Korea says,

00:28:01.279 --> 00:28:02.414
that's the model for us

00:28:02.414 --> 00:28:04.249
to take over the
entire peninsula

00:28:04.249 --> 00:28:07.052 align:start position:0%
and who knows who else is
thinking about things like this.

00:28:07.052 --> 00:28:09.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Is that our biggest
problem outside of our border

00:28:09.487 --> 00:28:13.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that if we don't do
anything, nobody else will?

00:28:13.258 --> 00:28:16.327
And sort of after 20 years
of failed nation building

00:28:16.327 --> 00:28:17.996
like the American
people, as he said,

00:28:17.996 --> 00:28:19.998
people don't want,
they certainly don't
want a ground war,

00:28:19.998 --> 00:28:22.133
I don't think they
want any sort of war,

00:28:22.133 --> 00:28:24.402 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't want a war, I
don't want to nation build,

00:28:24.402 --> 00:28:28.306
but I understand we do have
a unique place in the world.

00:28:28.306 --> 00:28:31.076
And if we're not the ones
kinda saying bad guys,

00:28:31.076 --> 00:28:32.877 align:start size:88% position:13%
you can't always do
whatever you want to do.

00:28:32.877 --> 00:28:34.679
It's like, then
all bets are off.

00:28:36.214 --> 00:28:37.382
- Yeah.

00:28:37.382 --> 00:28:38.917 align:start size:88% position:13%
Look, I mean, if you're
gonna be a country

00:28:38.917 --> 00:28:41.453
as big and as powerful with an
economy, as advanced as ours,

00:28:41.453 --> 00:28:43.588
then what happens around the
world is gonna matter to you

00:28:43.588 --> 00:28:45.023 align:start size:81% position:19%
because you're
sourcing products,

00:28:45.023 --> 00:28:48.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're sourcing raw material,
energy, trade, commerce

00:28:48.593 --> 00:28:50.095
with all over the world.

00:28:50.095 --> 00:28:51.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, you need
there to be stability.

00:28:51.629 --> 00:28:53.898
The one thing you can never
allow happen for example,

00:28:53.898 --> 00:28:55.233
is let's say China comes

00:28:55.233 --> 00:28:57.001 align:start size:84% position:16%
to dominate the
Asia-Pacific region

00:28:57.001 --> 00:28:58.837
and so, suddenly we can't trade,

00:28:58.837 --> 00:29:01.005 align:start size:88% position:13%
We've just lost all
those countries in Asia

00:29:01.005 --> 00:29:02.974
that we sell things to
and buy things from.

00:29:02.974 --> 00:29:05.043 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have to deal with
China and they decide what

00:29:05.043 --> 00:29:06.344
to sell or buy from us.

00:29:06.344 --> 00:29:07.645
So a lot of that
is really domestic.

00:29:07.645 --> 00:29:09.814
Like we have to have an
industrial capability

00:29:09.814 --> 00:29:10.749
in this country.

00:29:10.749 --> 00:29:11.683
We have to have the ability,

00:29:11.683 --> 00:29:12.917
you cannot be a great power

00:29:12.917 --> 00:29:14.486
if you're not an
industrial power.

00:29:14.486 --> 00:29:15.987
I don't care what
people tell you.

00:29:15.987 --> 00:29:18.690
You can't just be the place
that invents things on paper,

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.658
and then ships it off for
someone else to make it,

00:29:20.658 --> 00:29:22.393
eventually the people making
these things are the people

00:29:22.393 --> 00:29:23.728
that will invent them.

00:29:23.728 --> 00:29:24.963
And not to mention

00:29:24.963 --> 00:29:26.264
the national security
ramifications of it.

00:29:26.264 --> 00:29:27.665
So, number one,

00:29:27.665 --> 00:29:28.600
we have to be in
industrial power.

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:29.768
Number two, we have to make sure

00:29:29.768 --> 00:29:31.102
that we don't live in a world

00:29:31.102 --> 00:29:33.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
in which a great power
dominates a region.

00:29:33.438 --> 00:29:35.306
And basically we have
no access to people

00:29:35.306 --> 00:29:37.375
that want to trade
in commerce with us.

00:29:37.375 --> 00:29:39.043 align:start size:81% position:19%
That said, that
has to be balanced

00:29:39.043 --> 00:29:40.745
against the notion
of overextension.

00:29:40.745 --> 00:29:42.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
There are a lot of bad things
that happen in the world.

00:29:42.947 --> 00:29:45.083
We need to speak about
it and do what we can,

00:29:45.083 --> 00:29:47.919
but most of them do not have
an American military solution,

00:29:47.919 --> 00:29:50.221
nor should they, in terms
of us getting involved

00:29:50.221 --> 00:29:52.490
and overextending ourselves.

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:54.993
Our number one priority must
always be the national interest

00:29:54.993 --> 00:29:59.998 align:start size:97% position:3%
of our country and
protecting it from threats,

00:30:01.332 --> 00:30:02.534 align:start size:97% position:3%
both at home and our
interests around the world.

00:30:02.534 --> 00:30:05.136 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it's a balance
that they have

00:30:05.136 --> 00:30:06.604
to pull off in public policy.

00:30:06.604 --> 00:30:09.707 align:start size:97% position:3%
But to your greater point
about no one else will do it,

00:30:09.707 --> 00:30:11.543 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think a lot of
times what happens is

00:30:11.543 --> 00:30:13.111
if America isn't involved,

00:30:13.111 --> 00:30:15.413
then some people
will get involved,

00:30:15.413 --> 00:30:16.815 align:start size:84% position:16%
but they're gonna
get involved in ways

00:30:16.815 --> 00:30:19.350 align:start position:0%
that may not necessarily
further our national interests.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:21.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
The withdrawal from
Afghanistan, for example,

00:30:21.753 --> 00:30:23.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
has led a lot of countries
in the middle of it

00:30:23.388 --> 00:30:25.790
to increasingly turn
to China and Russia,

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:29.294
as their defense
partner preference.

00:30:29.294 --> 00:30:31.930
And that's concerning
because that could mean

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:36.267 align:start size:88% position:13%
for the future of
American economic
prosperity and security.

00:30:37.702 --> 00:30:40.271
- Do you think some of
this is sorta by design?

00:30:40.271 --> 00:30:42.607
Like when you see the
way a guy like say,

00:30:42.607 --> 00:30:44.909
Bill de Blasio kind of
wrecked New York city,

00:30:44.909 --> 00:30:46.477
which is just an
absolute disaster,

00:30:46.477 --> 00:30:48.446
a place that I lived
most of my adult life

00:30:48.446 --> 00:30:50.548
before moving to LA, you
know, people are like,

00:30:50.548 --> 00:30:53.785 align:start size:81% position:19%
oh, you know, this
is an accident.

00:30:53.785 --> 00:30:55.019
There're like, these policies,

00:30:55.019 --> 00:30:56.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
he didn't realize
what was gonna happen,

00:30:56.521 --> 00:30:58.423 align:start position:0%
but he just gave a speech a
couple days ago where it's like,

00:30:58.423 --> 00:31:00.758
oh no, this was great,
it's all great here.

00:31:00.758 --> 00:31:02.994
And I feel the sorta
similar way with Biden.

00:31:02.994 --> 00:31:04.462
It's like, I don't know

00:31:04.462 --> 00:31:05.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he really thinks
things aren't going well.

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:07.498
Now you look at the shelves

00:31:07.498 --> 00:31:09.367
and you look at people's
jobs and everything else,

00:31:09.367 --> 00:31:11.269 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you clearly realize
the price of gas,

00:31:11.269 --> 00:31:13.471
the price of food, et cetera,
you realize things aren't.

00:31:13.471 --> 00:31:16.107
But do you think some of
it is actually by design

00:31:16.107 --> 00:31:20.411
that it's a feature not a
bug in the way they govern.

00:31:20.411 --> 00:31:22.981 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Both, I think it's
the natural consequences

00:31:22.981 --> 00:31:23.915
of these decisions

00:31:23.915 --> 00:31:25.550
that always lead to disaster.

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:28.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I think they're
not necessarily overly
troubled by them.

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:30.421
Inflation is a great example.

00:31:30.421 --> 00:31:31.556
Inflation, if you believe

00:31:31.556 --> 00:31:34.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you want
Marxism and socialism

00:31:34.058 --> 00:31:37.095
and you want the government to
control more of our economy,

00:31:37.095 --> 00:31:39.664 align:start size:88% position:13%
then inflation is
necessarily a bad thing

00:31:39.664 --> 00:31:41.332
because the way you view it

00:31:41.332 --> 00:31:43.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
as the more expensive
life gets, right,

00:31:43.768 --> 00:31:45.036
the harder it is
to afford things,

00:31:45.036 --> 00:31:46.971
the harder it is
to get childcare,

00:31:46.971 --> 00:31:49.073 align:start size:97% position:3%
the harder, the more expensive
it is to go to school,

00:31:49.073 --> 00:31:51.910
the more people are going
to be open to the argument

00:31:51.910 --> 00:31:53.578
that these things cost too much.

00:31:53.578 --> 00:31:55.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
The only way you're
gonna be able

00:31:55.146 --> 00:31:58.049 align:start position:0%
to have it is that the
government provides it for you.

00:31:58.049 --> 00:31:59.417 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, once the
government begin there,

00:31:59.417 --> 00:32:00.585 align:start size:84% position:16%
the government
doesn't just take over

00:32:00.585 --> 00:32:02.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
early childhood
education, it decides,

00:32:02.787 --> 00:32:04.022
when it takes it over,

00:32:04.022 --> 00:32:05.623 line:0%
what's being taught and
who gets to teach it.

00:32:05.623 --> 00:32:07.725 line:0%
It doesn't just take
over higher education.

00:32:07.725 --> 00:32:10.261 line:0%
It decides what you have to
teach and who gets to go.

00:32:10.261 --> 00:32:11.429 line:0%
It doesn't just take over.

00:32:11.429 --> 00:32:13.031
When it sends you the cheque

00:32:13.031 --> 00:32:15.466
in the mail every
month, you know,

00:32:15.466 --> 00:32:18.469 align:start position:0%
it doesn't just decide how much
money you have in the bank.

00:32:18.469 --> 00:32:21.272
It makes you dependent on
that program continuing

00:32:21.272 --> 00:32:23.207
and having to increase
taxes on other people

00:32:23.207 --> 00:32:25.209
in order for the money
to be there, to do that.

00:32:25.209 --> 00:32:28.413 align:start size:97% position:3%
So in many ways, I think
this inflation is an example

00:32:28.413 --> 00:32:32.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
and even a supply
shortages create a scenario

00:32:32.083 --> 00:32:33.484
in which people they think,

00:32:33.484 --> 00:32:35.019
or become more open,

00:32:35.019 --> 00:32:36.654 align:start size:88% position:13%
they're becoming
dependent on government

00:32:36.654 --> 00:32:39.157 align:start size:97% position:3%
